<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
    res/layout/overlay_recording_catcher.xml
    ══════════════════════════════════════════
    A SEPARATE, full-screen WindowManager window from overlay_panel.xml.
    OverlayService only adds this to the screen while a recording session is
    active, and removes it the instant recording stops.

    WHY A SEPARATE FULL-SCREEN WINDOW (not just widening overlay_panel.xml):
      overlay_panel.xml's window is deliberately small and uses
      FLAG_NOT_TOUCH_MODAL, so touches OUTSIDE its small button bounds pass
      straight through to the app/game underneath — that's what lets the
      floating button coexist with normally using the game. Recording needs
      the OPPOSITE: every touch anywhere on screen must be seen by THIS app,
      not the one underneath. OverlayService builds this window's
      WindowManager.LayoutParams WITHOUT FLAG_NOT_TOUCH_MODAL — that single
      missing flag is what makes it touch-intercepting instead of
      pass-through. The direct, user-facing consequence: while this window
      is up, the game underneath receives ZERO taps. Recording and replay
      are separate phases, never simultaneous — the banner text below says
      this out loud so it's not just a code comment.

    THE DIM BACKGROUND:
      #33000000 = 20% opaque black (AA=33 in #AARRGGBB). Not fully
      transparent, on purpose — a visible tint is a constant on-screen
      reminder "you are in recording mode right now," reinforcing the
      tradeoff above visually instead of leaving it purely in documentation.
      Still light enough that the app/game underneath stays visible through
      it, so you can see what you're marking points on.

    TOUCH DISPATCH — WHY THE STOP BUTTON WORKS RELIABLY HERE:
      OverlayService attaches a setOnTouchListener to THIS layout's root
      view to capture every tap-down as a recorded point. The Button below
      is clickable=true by default, so standard Android touch dispatch
      hands it (not the root's listener) any touch that starts within its
      own bounds — the root's point-capture listener never sees a touch
      that lands on Stop Recording. This is why Stop Recording lives INSIDE
      this window's own view tree rather than as a separate floating
      control: it's the one place guaranteed reachable once this
      full-screen window covers everything else.
-->
<FrameLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/recordingCatcherRoot"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="#33000000">

    <!--
        Static instructional text, pinned near the top so it's visible
        regardless of screen size. Text itself lives in strings.xml
        (recording_banner_text) per this project's "no inline text" rule.
        A semi-opaque background behind the text keeps it legible over any
        app/game showing through the dim tint.
    -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/recordingBanner"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="top"
        android:background="#99000000"
        android:padding="16dp"
        android:text="@string/recording_banner_text"
        android:textColor="@color/white"
        android:textSize="14sp" />

    <!--
        Live count of points recorded so far, updated by OverlayService
        after every captured tap. Starts at "0 points recorded" via the
        same recording_points_counter format string used later for the
        stop Toast, so the wording is consistent between the two.
    -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/recordingPointCounter"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="top|center_horizontal"
        android:layout_marginTop="72dp"
        android:background="#99000000"
        android:padding="12dp"
        android:text="0 points recorded"
        android:textColor="@color/white"
        android:textSize="16sp"
        android:textStyle="bold" />

    <!--
        Ends the recording session. Bottom-center placement keeps it clear
        of the top banner/counter and reachable with a thumb regardless of
        where on screen points are being recorded.
    -->
    <Button
        android:id="@+id/stopRecordingButton"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="bottom|center_horizontal"
        android:layout_marginBottom="48dp"
        android:text="@string/stop_recording_button_text" />

</FrameLayout>
